EXT. PARKING GARAGE ROOFTOP - NIGHT

Concrete. Painted lines. A rusted rail overlooking a city of amber lights. A single black sedan sits under a dead lamp, engine ticking as it cools.

VERONIKA SALT, 40s, long coat, briefcase handcuffed to her left wrist, stands at the rail. She watches the stairwell door. Her breath fogs.

The door opens. CASSIAN BOYLE, 30s, bare head, shoulders tight in a gray suit, steps onto the roof alone. He carries nothing. He stops ten paces away.

VERONIKA
You're early.

CASSIAN
You're not.

VERONIKA
I've been here an hour.

CASSIAN
I know. I watched you from the street.

She smiles faintly. She shifts the briefcase. The chain between her wrist and the handle rattles.

VERONIKA
Then you know I came alone.

CASSIAN
I know you came with a gun in your boot.

VERONIKA
It's cold up here.

CASSIAN
It's always cold up here. That's why you picked it.

She lifts her chin. A car passes on the street below, headlights sliding across the wall. When the light fades, he has taken one step closer.

VERONIKA
Stop there.

CASSIAN
You said I could come close.

VERONIKA
I said you could come.

She steps backward until the rail presses against her spine. He takes another step. She slides a hand into her coat. He raises both his, palms open.

CASSIAN
Veronika. You called me.

VERONIKA
I did.

CASSIAN
You told me you had the files.

VERONIKA
I told you what you needed to hear.

CASSIAN
That's not an answer.

VERONIKA
It's the only one I have.

He studies her. His shoulders ease. He lowers his hands.

CASSIAN
You don't have them.

VERONIKA
No.

CASSIAN
You never did.

VERONIKA
I never said I did.

CASSIAN
Then why drag me up here?

VERONIKA
Because I wanted to see your face when you found out.

CASSIAN
And?

VERONIKA
It's disappointing.

He laughs, short and dry. She draws a small pistol from her coat and trains it on his chest. He stops laughing. The garage hums.

CASSIAN
Veronika.

VERONIKA
Cassian.

CASSIAN ^
Put the gun down.

VERONIKA
Put the gun down, or I'll count to three.

He glances at the stairwell door. She follows his gaze. The moment she looks away he lunges, knocking the pistol aside with the flat of his hand and slamming her into the railing. The briefcase swings on its chain and strikes his hip. They fall in a heap on the concrete, grappling, her free hand clawing at his collar, his forearm across her throat.

VERONIKA (CONT'D)
(gasping)
Cassian. Look up.

He does. Across the roof, three red dots of laser light settle on his chest. He freezes. His arm loosens. She slips out from under him, picks up the pistol, and stands.

CASSIAN
You called them.

VERONIKA
I called everyone.

She unlocks the briefcase handcuff with a key from her sleeve and drops the case at his feet. The lid is open. It is empty.

VERONIKA (CONT'D)
That's all there ever was.

She walks to the stairwell door. At the threshold, she looks back. He stays kneeling on the concrete in the red light, hands open, and says nothing. She goes out. The door closes.
